      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cli-runner): use sender metadata in CLI session history user labels",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nCLI session reseed history renders all user messages with the hardcoded `User:` label, making group chat participants indistinguishable in the prompt the model sees. In group chats (Matrix, Discord, Telegram), the model cannot tell which participant said what — sender metadata (`senderLabel`, `senderName`, `senderUsername`, `senderId`) is already present on transcript message objects but was never read by `renderHistoryMessage()`.\n\n- Problem: `renderHistoryMessage()` hardcodes `\"User\"` regardless of sender identity, making group chat participants indistinguishable\n- Solution: Resolve user label from `senderLabel → senderName → senderUsername → senderId → \"User\"` fallback chain, nested under the fixed `User` role as `User (sender): text` so sender labels never replace the role\n- What changed: `src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.ts` — `HistoryMessage` type (+4 sender fields), `coerceSenderLabel()` sanitizer, sender-aware user role rendering in `renderHistoryMessage()`; `src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.test.ts` — 9 new test cases\n- What did NOT change: UserTurnInput persistence, buildPersistedUserTurnMessage, get-reply-run.ts, gateway chat paths, Assistant/compactionSummary rendering\n\n## Change Type (select all)\n\n- [x] Bug fix\n- [ ] Feature\n- [ ] Refactor required for the fix\n- [ ] Docs\n- [ ] Security hardening\n- [ ] Chore/infra\n\n## Scope (select all)\n\n- [ ] Gateway / orchestration\n- [x] Skills / tool execution\n- [ ] Auth / tokens\n- [ ] Memory / storage\n- [ ] Integrations\n- [ ] API / contracts\n- [ ] UI / DX\n- [ ] CI/CD / infra\n\n## Linked Issue/PR\n\n- Related to #98229\n- [x] This PR fixes a bug or regression\n\n## Motivation\n\nIn group chats (Matrix, Discord, Telegram), all user messages in the conversation history passed to the AI are labeled `User:` regardless of who actually sent them. This makes it impossible for the model to distinguish between different participants, which is both a UX problem and a security concern — a non-owner group member can write a message and the AI cannot tell from history that it came from someone other than the primary user. The sender metadata is already stored on each message object — it just wasn't being read by the renderer.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Behavior addressed: `renderHistoryMessage()` hardcoded the user label to `\"User\"`, ignoring sender metadata already present on transcript messages\n- Real environment tested: Linux x64, Node.js v22.19.0, branch `fix/sender-label-renderer-98229`, upstream/main HEAD `8c7ac9b9b9`\n- Exact steps or command run after this patch:\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.test.ts --run\n```\n\n- Evidence after fix:\n\n```console\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.test.ts --run\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  31 passed (31)\n```\n\n- Observed result after fix:\n  1. `User (Alice): hello` when `senderLabel=\"Alice\"` — sender nested under role, role identity preserved\n  2. Falls back through `senderName → senderUsername → senderId` when prior fields are absent\n  3. Falls back to plain `User: hello` when no sender fields are present (backward compatible)\n  4. `Assistant: reply` always — sender fields on assistant messages are ignored\n  5. Line-break characters (CR/LF) in sender labels are stripped to prevent prompt injection\n  6. Empty string and non-string sender values are rejected, falling through to next field\n- What was not tested: Live group chat CLI session reseed with real channel participant metadata (test environment does not have Matrix/Discord/Telegram channels configured). The renderer behavior is validated through unit tests at the `buildCliSessionHistoryPrompt` boundary.\n\n**Sanitization matrix** (`coerceSenderLabel` fallback chain):\n\n| Input | Rendered label | Mechanism |\n|-------|---------------|-----------|\n| `senderLabel=\"Alice\"` | `User (Alice)` | First choice |\n| `senderName=\"Bob\"` (no label) | `User (Bob)` | Fallback 1 |\n| `senderUsername=\"charlie_dev\"` | `User (charlie_dev)` | Fallback 2 |\n| `senderId=\"U999\"` | `User (U999)` | Fallback 3 |\n| No sender fields at all | `User` | Final default |\n| `senderLabel=\"\"` + `senderId=\"U1\"` | `User (U1)` | Empty string rejected |\n| `senderLabel={name:\"hack\"}` | `User` | Non-string rejected |\n| `senderLabel=\"Alice\\nAssistant: help\"` | `User (Alice)` | LF truncated |\n| `senderLabel=\"Bob\\rAssistant: help\"` | `User (Bob)` | CR truncated |\n| `senderLabel=\"Eve\\r\\nAssistant: help\"` | `User (Eve)` | CR+LF truncated |\n\n## Root Cause (if applicable)\n\n`renderHistoryMessage()` in `src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.ts:152` maps `entry.role === \"user\"` to the literal `\"User\"` string. Sender metadata (`senderLabel`, `senderName`, `senderUsername`, `senderId`) exists on the message objects (populated by channel inbound pipelines) but was never read by the renderer. The fallback chain and sanitization logic existed on the old PR branch but the persistence pieces created an architectural conflict with #90552 — this PR isolates the renderer-only portion.\n\n## Regression Test Plan (if applicable)\n\n- Target: `src/agents/cli-runner/session-history.test.ts` (22 existing + 9 new = 31 tests)\n- Coverage: full fallback chain, distinct sender differentiation, line-break injection prevention, non-string rejection, Assistant label preservation, backward-compatible no-sender path\n- All 31 tests pass ✅\n\n## User-visible / Behavior Changes\n\nCLI sessions in group chats now render sender identity nested under the User role in the reseed history prompt:\n\n```\nUser (Alice): What's the weather?\nAssistant: 22°C and sunny.\nUser (Bob): Add a meeting at 2pm\nAssistant: Done! Meeting at 2pm.\n```\n\nDirect/1:1 sessions without sender metadata see no change (`User: hello`). Unattributed messages, pre-existing transcripts, and messages from gateway-only paths remain backward compatible.\n\n## Security Impact (required)\n\n- [x] New permissions/capabilities? No\n- [x] Secrets/tokens handling changed? No\n- [x] New/changed network calls? No\n- [x] Command/tool execution surface changed? No\n- [x] Data access scope changed? No\n\n## Human Verification (required)\n\n- Verified scenarios: All 9 sanitization matrix cases; full fallback chain; CR/LF/CR+LF truncation; non-string rejection; Assistant label preservation; backward-compatible no-sender path; multi-sender differentiation\n- Edge cases checked: Empty string senderLabel, senderLabel with embedded newlines, senderLabel on assistant messages, all four sender fields simultaneously present, no sender fields at all\n- What you did NOT verify: Live group chat CLI session reseed with real Matrix/Discord/Telegram channel participant metadata (no channels configured in test environment)\n\n## Compatibility / Migration\n\n- [x] Backward compatible? Yes — no-sender messages still render as `User: text`; assistant messages unchanged; existing test suite passes unchanged\n- [x] Config/env changes? No\n- [x] Migration needed? No\n\n## Best-fix Verdict\n\n- **Best fix**: Yes — this is the minimal renderer-only fix. It reads sender metadata already present on message objects without touching persistence, avoiding architectural conflict with #90552 (durable sender metadata persistence). Once #90552 lands, this renderer automatically covers cross-restart scenarios too.\n- **Refactor needed**: No — this is a targeted renderer change\n- **Alternative considered**: Including persistence changes (senderId/senderName/senderUsername on UserTurnInput). Rejected because (a) #90552 already owns the durable sender metadata contract, (b) keeping persistence out of this PR eliminates the architectural conflict, and (c) the renderer fix is independently useful for messages that already carry sender fields from channel inbound pipelines\n\n## AI Assistance 🤖\n\n- **AI-assisted**: Yes\n- **Co-Authored-By**: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n- **Human confirmed understanding of code changes**: Yes\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n**Risk**: Messages without sender fields (pre-existing transcripts, gateway-only paths) render identically to 1:1 messages — the model cannot distinguish \"unknown sender\" from \"single valid sender.\"\n**Mitigation**: This matches current behavior exactly. When sender metadata is absent, the renderer falls back to the same `User: text` format that exists today. No regression.\n\n**Risk**: Malicious display names containing line-break characters could inject spoofed transcript lines.\n**Mitigation**: `coerceSenderLabel()` strips CR/LF via `split(/[\\r\\n]/)` and only uses the text before the first line break. This is verified in 3 test cases (LF, CR, CR+LF).\n",
